module dev.slatelang.slate

import sh.sysl.parsing.*
import sysl.raw.ptr_cast
import sysl.buf.{Buf, buf}
import sysl.container.{Map, map}
import sysl.posix.tty.{flush}
import sysl.text.{from_utf8_unchecked, str_builder, Search, StrBuilder}

// The functions a slate program has without writing them.
//
// **Each is an ordinary value bound in the root scope**, so `val p = print` binds it and
// `apply(print, 1)` calls it. A builtin dispatched on the name at the call site would have been the
// one kind of function a program could not pass around, which in a language where functions are
// values is a hole rather than a simplification.
//
// **The LIST of them is in `natives.sysl` and how a value is PRINTED is in `render.sysl`.** What is
// here is the dispatch and the implementations: `call_native` and the `do_*` functions it reaches.
// Nothing private crosses either cut in either direction, which is what made both of them the cuts
// to make.
//
// **TCP and the file system are `builtin_io.sysl`**, cut out when this file passed a thousand lines:
// their `n_*` ids, `install_net`, `install_fs` and the `do_*` behind them. Nothing private crosses
// that cut either, a builtin being reached through the registry's table.
//
// **The four a test is written with are `assert.sysl`**, cut out on the section heading that was
// already here when this file passed a thousand lines. Those four cross -- `run_native` calls
// them by name -- and they are the four that answer whether a test passed rather than answering a
// value.
// Everything the program printed, in order, is `current().output`.
//
// **A field of the VM (`vm_state.sysl`), which is what a second interpreter in one process needs.**
// It was module storage until then, because a root function has to be a top-level function to have
// an address and has to reach the interpreter's state -- so that state could not be a local of
// anything. `run` clears it, which is what lets two programs run one after the other.

// Whether `print` also reaches the real descriptor as each call happens, rather than only building
// up `output` for whoever asked to run this program to replay once the whole thing has finished.
//
// **Off by default, because most callers of `run` are asking a question rather than running a
// program.** A test that executes a snippet of slate to check what it printed wants the answer as a
// value, not scattered across the gate's own terminal thousands of times over -- and the doc-test
// harness compares `output` to a fenced block, which a stream already consumed cannot be compared
// against. `run_program` in `main.sysl` turns this on because it is not asking a question: a program
// run from the command line may never return, and a supervisor's first line has to reach a reader
// before its hundredth line does. It is `current().live_output`.

// Where a live `print` goes: `main.sysl`'s `say`, which is `run_cli`'s caller's choice of where the
// driver's own words go. A test drives the CLI in-process with a sink of its own rather than the
// real descriptor (`cli_said` in `tests_kit_cli.sysl`), and a program run through it is reached by
// `run_program` exactly as a real invocation is -- so what `live_output` turns on has to call
// WHATEVER `say` this run was given, not the real `print`, or a captured run would say nothing and a
// captured run's sink would never see what its own program printed.
//
// Not `private`: `run_program` in `main.sysl` puts `live_sink` back to this once the run it started
// is over.
no_live_sink(line: string) = ()

// The sink itself is `current().live_sink`.

// Whether the test now running asked to be left out, and the reason it gave, are
// `current().skip_asked` and `current().skip_reason`.
//
// **Read by the runner rather than matched on the signal, and the asynchronous case is why.** A
// `skip` inside an `async` test travels out of the coroutine and settles its promise as a rejection,
// which keeps the sentence and loses which variant it was -- so the fact has to be written down where
// `skip` was called. `run_one_test` clears both before every test.

// **EVERY ARGUMENT IS ROOTED FOR THE LENGTH OF THE CALL BY STANDING ON THE OPERAND STACK, WHICH IS
// WHAT MAKES THIS FUNCTION EMPTY OF ROOTING.** The arguments used to be popped into a sysl `Buf`,
// which the collector does not walk, so the dispatch held every one of them on the shadow stack and
// released them afterwards. They are left where the instruction put them instead: the stack is a root
// the tracer already walks, so a builtin that calls back into slate -- `map` walks a receiver and
// calls a function -- cannot have its receiver or its callback collected under it.
//
// **What the old hole looked like was never a memory failure.** `["a", "b", "c"].map(r -> burn(500))`
// failed with *"this array was changed while `map` was walking it"* about a literal nothing can
// reach; binding the array to a `val` moved the failure onto the callback -- *"this function takes
// from 1 to 0 arguments"* -- because the freed slot had been taken by something else and read back as
// a function of a different arity.
//
// **THE CALLER CUTS THE ARGUMENTS OFF, NOT THIS.** They have to outlive the call and they have to go
// once it is over, and only the caller knows what else is standing under them -- the callee on the
// ordinary path, the receiver on a method's, nothing at all where a `Buf` was pushed for the
// occasion.
call_native(f: NativeFn, args: Args, at: Span) -> Step
    // **Two clock readings, or none at all where the profiler is off** -- `profile_began` answers
    // zero without reading a clock, and `profile_native` returns on the same flag. A builtin that
    // runs a slate callback is timed inclusively; `profile.sysl` says so.
    //
    // **The flag is read once, here, and not again on the way out**: it is thread-local, so each
    // read is a `_tlv_get_addr` call, and the instrument being off is the case every program runs.
    if !profile_on then return run_native(f, args, at)

    val began = profile_began()
    val answer = run_native(f, args, at)

    profile_native(f, began)
    answer
end call_native

// Call a builtin whose arguments are in a `Buf` rather than standing on the stack.
//
// **The values go onto the operand stack and are cut off again**, which is what every caller that has
// not got them on the stack already owes: `apply` and the callback path, a property read, a spread. It
// is the same buffer the old dispatch made, moved to the handful of callers that genuinely have one
// instead of being made on every builtin call in the program.
//
// **The cut is skipped after a park**, exactly as `run_chunk`'s is and for its reason: a suspended
// call swapped the running machine for a fresh one, so the height recorded here belongs to a stack
// that is no longer the one standing.
call_native_buf(f: NativeFn, vals: Buf[Value], at: Span) -> Step
    val running = current()
    val vm = &running.machine
    val base = vm.stack.len()

    for i in 0..<vals.len()
        vm.stack.push(vals.at(i))

    val answer = call_native(f, Args(running, base, vals.len()), at)

    answer match
        Err(Wait) -> ()
        _ -> vm.stack.truncate(base)

    answer
end call_native_buf

// -- what this file registers ---------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// **Four groups, and each one is here because its implementations are.** `private` in sysl is
// private to the FILE, so the `do_*` below can only be registered from inside it -- which is the
// whole shape of the rule: a subsystem's natives, the block that registers them and the scope they
// are declared into stay together, and no central list has to learn a new name.

// The handful that belong to no module: what a program prints with, what every value answers to, and
// the four object operations.
var n_print: NativeFn = 0

// **Characters, not UTF-16 units.** Reached only as `.length` on a string, an array and a range --
// there is no global `len`, the property being the one spelling.
var n_length: NativeFn = 0

var n_push: NativeFn = 0

// **The type words double as the conversions**, so `string` tests in a pattern and converts in an
// expression.
var n_string: NativeFn = 0

// **The four universal methods**, reached on any value that has not put a field of the same name in
// front of them. `method.sysl` says why they are the only names allowed to do that.
var n_to_string: NativeFn = 0
var n_eq: NativeFn = 0
var n_ne: NativeFn = 0
var n_equals: NativeFn = 0

var n_keys: NativeFn = 0
var n_has: NativeFn = 0

// **`without` and not `remove`**, which `slate:fs` took for deleting a file. An object is not
// changed, so the participle is right anyway: `sorted` answers a new array and this answers a new
// object.
var n_without: NativeFn = 0

// **`freeze` and `isFrozen` are JavaScript's names and the SHALLOW rule is JavaScript's too.** They
// sit beside `without` because they are the same tier: an operation on a container a program built,
// wanted by the same programs and reached without importing anything.
var n_freeze: NativeFn = 0
var n_is_frozen: NativeFn = 0

install_basics(env: *EnvObj)
    n_print = register("print", do_print)
    n_length = register("length", do_len)
    n_push = register("push", do_push)
    n_string = register("string", do_string)
    n_to_string = register("toString", do_to_string)
    n_eq = register("eq", (args, at) -> do_eq(args, at, true))
    n_ne = register("ne", (args, at) -> do_eq(args, at, false))
    n_equals = register("equals", do_equals)
    n_keys = register("keys", do_keys)
    n_has = register("has", do_has)
    n_without = register("without", do_without)
    n_freeze = register("freeze", do_freeze)
    n_is_frozen = register("isFrozen", do_is_frozen)

    declare(env, "print", Native(n_print), false)
    declare(env, "push", Native(n_push), false)
    declare(env, "string", Native(n_string), false)
    declare(env, "keys", Native(n_keys), false)

    // **The other two ways to walk an object.** `entries` is what makes a destructuring `for` head
    // worth having -- `for [k, v] in entries(o)` is the loop every program over a record wants.
    declare(env, "has", Native(n_has), false)

    // **The one object operation there was no way to write.** `keys`, `values`, `entries` and `has`
    // read, and `with` writes -- so a table that had to forget something had to be rebuilt by hand
    // out of everything else, which is what `slate-language/sluice`'s session store does.
    declare(env, "without", Native(n_without), false)

    declare(env, "freeze", Native(n_freeze), false)
    declare(env, "isFrozen", Native(n_is_frozen), false)
end install_basics

// The event loop's, which are node's names because a program that knows node should not have to
// learn new ones for the same thing.
var n_set_timeout: NativeFn = 0
var n_set_interval: NativeFn = 0
var n_clear_timeout: NativeFn = 0

// Promises. `sleep` is the one that makes `await` worth having on its own -- everything else a
// program can build out of an async function.
var n_sleep: NativeFn = 0
var n_resolve: NativeFn = 0
var n_reject: NativeFn = 0

// **A promise nobody has answered yet, and the two ways of answering it.** `resolve` and `reject`
// make a promise that has already settled, which is the easy half; these are the half that bridges a
// callback into `await`, and without them a program can only wait for something slate itself
// started. TC39's `Promise.withResolvers` is the same three pieces.
var n_pending: NativeFn = 0
var n_settle: NativeFn = 0
var n_fail: NativeFn = 0

install_timers(env: *EnvObj)
    n_set_timeout = register("setTimeout", (args, at) -> do_timer(args, at, false))
    n_set_interval = register("setInterval", (args, at) -> do_timer(args, at, true))
    n_clear_timeout = register("clearTimeout", do_clear)
    n_sleep = register("sleep", do_sleep)
    n_resolve = register("resolve", (args, at) -> do_settled(args, at, false))
    n_reject = register("reject", (args, at) -> do_settled(args, at, true))
    n_pending = register("pending", do_pending)
    n_settle = register("settle", (args, at) -> do_answer(args, at, false))
    n_fail = register("fail", (args, at) -> do_answer(args, at, true))

    declare(env, "setTimeout", Native(n_set_timeout), false)
    declare(env, "setInterval", Native(n_set_interval), false)
    declare(env, "clearTimeout", Native(n_clear_timeout), false)

    // node has both names for one operation, and so does slate.
    declare(env, "clearInterval", Native(n_clear_timeout), false)

    // **Bare names where JavaScript has `Promise.resolve` and `Promise.reject`.** slate has no
    // namespaces yet, and inventing one for three functions would be inventing it for the wrong
    // reason.
    declare(env, "sleep", Native(n_sleep), false)
    declare(env, "resolve", Native(n_resolve), false)
    declare(env, "reject", Native(n_reject), false)
    declare(env, "pending", Native(n_pending), false)
    declare(env, "settle", Native(n_settle), false)
    declare(env, "fail", Native(n_fail), false)
end install_timers

// Whether these arguments begin with an array, which is what tells the array form of a shared name
// from the text one. A wrong first argument falls through to the text form and is complained about
// there, where the message already names what it wanted.
// Not `private`: `text.sysl` registers `contains`, `indexOf` and `lastIndexOf`, the three names that
// answer about an array or about a string, and this is what tells the two apart.
first_is_array(args: Args) -> bool
    if args.is_empty() then return false

    args.at(0) match
        Array(_) -> true
        _ -> false
end first_is_array

// Whether these arguments begin with a buffer of bytes, which is the third reading the three shared
// names have: `indexOf` finds a byte or a run of bytes in one, where the array form compares values
// and the string form compares characters.
first_is_bytes(args: Args) -> bool
    if args.is_empty() then return false

    args.at(0) match
        Bytes(_) -> true
        _ -> false
end first_is_bytes


// `sleep(ms)` -- a promise for later.
private do_sleep(args: Args, at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 1 then return fault(at, s"`sleep` takes a number of milliseconds and was given ${counted(args.len(), "argument")}")

    args.at(0) match
        Int(n) -> sleep_promise(n, at)
        Real(r) -> sleep_promise(long(r), at)

        // **A duration says what a bare number cannot**, and is why `sleep(2.seconds())` reads
        // better than `sleep(2000)`. The number form stays, being what node writes and what every
        // program here already says.
        DurationVal(us) -> sleep_promise(us / 1000, at)
        other -> fault(at, s"`sleep` takes a number of milliseconds or a duration, and this is ${other.type_name()}")
end do_sleep

// `resolve(v)` and `reject(message)` -- a promise that has already settled.
//
// **Worth having even though an `async` function makes both**, because the two are what a program
// reaches for when it is adapting something that is not async at all: a cache hit that needs to look
// like a lookup, a validation that fails before any work starts.
private do_settled(args: Args, at: Span, failing: bool) -> Step
    if args.len() != 1
        val what = if failing then "`reject`" else "`resolve`"

        return fault(at, s"${what} takes one argument and was given ${args.len()}")

    val mark = root_mark()
    val pr = new_promise()

    hold(Promise(pr))

    // **A rejection raises the same fault `throw` raises**, which is `thrown_payload`'s rule read one
    // level out: `reject("gone")` is a sentence and `reject(NotFound(3))` is a value a `catch` can
    // sort on, or the two ways of raising a fault would answer differently after an `await`.
    if failing then settle_thrown(pr, thrown_message(at, args.at(0)), at, current().current_source, thrown_payload(args.at(0))) else settle_ok(pr, args.at(0))

    release(mark)
    Ok(Promise(pr))
end do_settled

// `pending()` -- a promise nobody has answered yet -- and `settle(p, v)` and `fail(p, message)`,
// which answer it.
//
// **This is the bridge from a callback into `await`.** `resolve` and `reject` adapt something that
// has already happened; these adapt something that has not, which is the case a program cannot write
// its way out of: without them the only things awaitable are the ones slate itself started, and
// anything with a callback of its own -- a library that calls back when a download finishes, a queue
// handing out a job, the end of a request body -- stays in callbacks and cannot be awaited at all.
//
// **A promise settles once, and a second answer is dropped rather than refused.** That is
// `settle_ok`'s rule rather than a new one, and it is JavaScript's: two callbacks racing to answer
// one promise is a program deciding late, not a program in error.
private do_pending(args: Args, at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 0
        return fault(at, s"`pending` takes no arguments and was given ${counted(args.len(), "argument")}")

    Ok(Promise(new_promise()))
end do_pending

private do_answer(args: Args, at: Span, failing: bool) -> Step
    val name = if failing then "fail" else "settle"

    if args.len() != 2
        return fault(at, s"`${name}` takes a promise and an answer, and was given ${counted(args.len(), "argument")}")

    val pr = args.at(0) match
        Promise(p) -> p
        other -> return fault(at, s"`${name}` answers a promise, and this is ${other.type_name()}")

    if failing then settle_thrown(pr, thrown_message(at, args.at(1)), at, current().current_source, thrown_payload(args.at(1))) else settle_ok(pr, args.at(1))

    Ok(Null)
end do_answer

// `setTimeout(fn, ms)`; the delay may be left out, and means the next turn of the loop.
private do_timer(args: Args, at: Span, repeating: bool) -> Step
    if args.len() == 0 || args.len() > 2
        return fault(at, s"a timer takes a function and a delay, and was given ${counted(args.len(), "argument")}")

    val ms = if args.len() == 2
        args.at(1) match
            Int(n) -> n
            Real(r) -> long(r)
            DurationVal(us) -> us / 1000
            other -> return fault(at, s"a delay is a number of milliseconds or a duration, and this is ${other.type_name()}")
    else
        0

    set_timer(args.at(0), ms, repeating, at)
end do_timer

private do_clear(args: Args, at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 1 then return fault(at, s"cancelling a timer takes its id, and was given ${counted(args.len(), "argument")}")

    clear_timer(args.at(0), at)

// `print(a, b, ...)` -- every argument rendered, a space between, one line.
//
// **Reaches `live_sink` at once when `live_output` is set, flushed behind it.** `putbytes` goes
// through C's `putchar`, and C block-buffers a stream that is not a terminal -- so a program piped
// to a file or a supervisor's journal would say nothing until it exited, which a program that never
// exits says never. `flush()` is one syscall per line, the same price node pays on every
// `console.log`.
private do_print(args: Args, at: Span) -> Step
    var out = str_builder()

    for i in 0..<args.len()
        if i > 0 then out.push_char(' ')

        out.push(printed(args.at(i), at)?)

    val line = out.finish()

    val v = current()

    v.output.push(line)

    if v.live_output
        v.live_sink(line)
        flush()

    Ok(Null)
end do_print

// `.length` -- of an array, a string or a range. An object counts its keys through `keys(o).length`
// instead, `.length` being a property no name on an object may be given.
private do_len(args: Args, at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 1 then return fault(at, s"`.length` takes one argument and was given ${args.len()}")

    args.at(0) match
        Array(a) -> Ok(Int(long(a.items.len())))

        // **Bytes, because that is what a buffer holds.** This is the number a `Content-Length` and
        // every length prefix on a wire is, which is why `toBytes(s).length` has always been how a
        // slate program asks for it.
        Bytes(b) -> Ok(Int(long(b.data.len())))
        // **Characters, not bytes.** `"日本語".length` is three; it answered nine until the day the
        // rule was settled, and every slice taken from that count cut a character in half.
        // The cell carries the number, so this is a read and not a walk -- which is what a `while i
        // < s.length` over a long string depends on.
        Str(p) -> Ok(Int(long(str_count(p))))

        // How many whole numbers it covers, which is exactly how many turns a `for` over it takes.
        RangeVal(r) ->
            if !r.has_lo || !r.has_hi
                return fault(at, "a range with an end left out has no length until it is used on something")

            Ok(Int(long(range_count(r.lo, r.hi, r.inclusive, r.step))))
        other -> fault(at, s"`.length` needs an array, bytes, a string or a range, and this is ${other.type_name()}")
end do_len

// `push(xs, v)` -- onto the end of an array, which it changes.
//
// **It answers nothing, as every mutator here does.** Handing the array back is what JavaScript does
// and is what makes `xs.push(1).push(2)` read like a pipeline over fresh values when it is two
// writes to one array; `array.sysl` carries the rule and the rest of the family.
private do_push(args: Args, at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 2 then return fault(at, s"`push` takes two arguments and was given ${args.len()}")

    args.at(0) match
        Array(a) ->
            if a.frozen then return fault(at, frozen_array_complaint("push"))

            keepable(args.at(1), at, "stored")?
            array_push(a, args.at(1))
            Ok(Null)

        // **ONE BYTE OR A WHOLE BUFFER, and the second is the reason this kind exists.** Appending
        // one buffer to another used to be a loop in the program, one `Value` per byte; here it is
        // an `extend`, which is a copy of a run of memory. A number is a byte and must be one --
        // `push(b, 256)` is a mistake in the program, not a truncation.
        Bytes(b) -> if b.frozen then fault(at, frozen_bytes_complaint("push")) else args.at(1) match
            Int(_) ->
                bytes_push(b, byte_of_value(args.at(1), at)?)
                Ok(Null)
            Bytes(other) ->
                // **`push(b, b)` IS COPIED FIRST, and that is not fussiness**: `extend` reads a view
                // into the very storage it is growing, which a reallocation moves out from under it.
                val ba: *u8 = ptr_cast(b)
                val oa: *u8 = ptr_cast(other)

                if ba == oa
                    var copy: Buf[u8] = buf()

                    copy.extend(other.data.view())
                    bytes_extend(b, copy.view())
                else
                    bytes_extend(b, other.data.view())

                Ok(Null)
            other -> fault(at, s"`push` onto bytes takes a byte or more bytes, and this is ${other.type_name()}")
        other -> fault(at, s"`push` needs an array or bytes, and this is ${other.type_name()}")
end do_push

// `str(x)` -- what `print` would have shown.
private do_string(args: Args, at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 1 then return fault(at, s"`string` takes one argument and was given ${args.len()}")

    printed_value(args.at(0), at)

// `v.toString()` -- what `print` would show, and what a class may say for itself.
//
// **Reached only where the value has no `toString` of its own**, the universal table being the last
// thing a method lookup tries -- so this never recurses into the hook that would have replaced it.
private do_to_string(args: Args, at: Span) -> Step
    val extra = past_receiver("toString", args, at)?

    if extra != 0 then return fault(at, s"`toString` takes no arguments and was given ${counted(extra, "argument")}")

    Ok(str_value(printed(args.at(0), at)?))

// `a.eq(b)` and `a.ne(b)` -- the same OBJECT, not the same shape.
private do_eq(args: Args, at: Span, want: bool) -> Step
    val name = if want then "eq" else "ne"
    val extra = past_receiver(name, args, at)?

    if extra != 1 then return fault(at, s"`${name}` takes one argument and was given ${counted(extra, "argument")}")

    Ok(Bool(identical(args.at(0), args.at(1), at) == want))

// `a.equals(b)` -- `==` under its own name, hooks and all.
//
// **The two may not disagree**, which is why this is `same` rather than a second reading of it: a
// class that writes an `equals` changes both at once, and a class that writes none gets identity from
// both because that is what `same` now does for a nominal value.
private do_equals(args: Args, at: Span) -> Step
    val extra = past_receiver("equals", args, at)?

    if extra != 1 then return fault(at, s"`equals` takes one argument and was given ${counted(extra, "argument")}")

    Ok(Bool(same(args.at(0), args.at(1), at)?))

// `keys(o)` -- an object's field names, as an array of strings.
//
// **An array's keys are its POSITIONS**, `[0, 1, 2]`, which is JavaScript's `Array.prototype.keys`
// and is the third of the trio `values` and `entries` already answer for an array.
private do_keys(args: Args, at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 1 then return fault(at, s"`keys` takes one argument and was given ${args.len()}")

    args.at(0) match
        Object(o) ->
            // In the order the fields were first written, which the table preserves.
            Ok(Array(new_array(obj_keys(o))))
        Array(xs) ->
            var out: Buf[Value] = buf()

            for i in 0..<xs.items.len()
                out.push(Int(long(i)))

            Ok(Array(new_array(out)))
        other -> fault(at, s"`keys` needs an object or an array, and this is ${other.type_name()}")
end do_keys

// Whether an object has a key at all.
//
// **This is the only way to tell an absent field from one holding `null`.** slate has one absent value
// where JavaScript has two, so reading answers `null` either way -- and the recovery is to ask the
// question rather than to read and inspect. Python draws the line in the same place with `in`, and Go
// with the second result of a map read.
private do_has(args: Args, at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 2 then return fault(at, s"`has` takes an object and a key, and was given ${counted(args.len(), "argument")}")

    args.at(0) match
        // **A key the walks hide is a key the object does not have**, so `has(p, "proto")` on a class
        // instance is false where `keys(p)` no longer lists it. Two answers to one question is what
        // the four walks had between them before this.
        Object(o) ->
            if !walked_key(o, args.at(1)) then return Ok(Bool(false))

            Ok(Bool(obj_has(o, args.at(1), at)?))
        other -> fault(at, s"`has` needs an object, and this is ${other.type_name()}")
end do_has

// `without(o, key)` -- the object again, with that key gone.
//
// **A NEW object, because a slate object's shape is not a thing a program changes in place.** `with`
// is the other half of this and answers a copy for the same reason; a `delete` writing through the
// value would make every name holding it see the field go, which is the surprise `with` was built to
// avoid.
//
// **A key that is not there is not an error**, so `without` is idempotent and a program removing a
// key it is unsure of writes one line. **A data value's copy is a data value**, exactly as `with`'s
// is -- and a hidden key cannot be removed, there being nothing there to a program that asks.
private do_without(args: Args, at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 2 then return fault(at, s"`without` takes an object and a key, and was given ${counted(args.len(), "argument")}")

    args.at(0) match
        Object(fields) ->
            val key = args.at(1)
            val mark = root_mark()
            val o = new_object()

            hold(Object(o))

            for i in 0..<entry_count(fields)
                val e = entry_at(fields, i)

                if !e.dead && !(walked_key(fields, e.key) && same(e.key, key, at)?)
                    obj_put(o, e.key, e.value, at)?

            // Set last, the writes above being how the copy was filled -- `WithObject`'s own
            // ordering and for its reason, including that a copy of a value `freeze` closed is an
            // ordinary object.
            o.data_value = fields.data_value
            o.frozen = fields.data_value

            release(mark)
            Ok(Object(o))
        other -> fault(at, s"`without` needs an object, and this is ${other.type_name()}")
end do_without

// `freeze(v)` -- close a container to writing, and answer the very value it was given.
//
// **IT IS SHALLOW, WHICH IS JavaScript's RULE AND IS THE ONE WORTH STATING.** Freezing an object
// says nothing about the objects its fields hold; a program wanting the whole graph walks it
// itself. A deep freeze cannot be the default here for the reason it is not there either -- the
// graph may be cyclic, may be large, and may hold values the program does not own.
//
// **A value that is not a container ANSWERS ITSELF**, again JavaScript's rule: a number cannot be
// written through, so there is nothing for `freeze` to do and nothing to refuse. That makes
// `freeze` safe to write over a value whose kind the program does not know.
//
// **Freezing twice is not an error.** The flag is already true and the call is a no-op, which is
// what lets a constructor freeze what it was handed without asking whether somebody else did.
private do_freeze(args: Args, at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 1 then return fault(at, s"`freeze` takes one value, and was given ${counted(args.len(), "argument")}")

    val v = args.at(0)

    v match
        Object(o) -> o.frozen = true
        Array(a) -> a.frozen = true
        Bytes(b) -> b.frozen = true

        // **A set, a map and a weak map ARE the object's table**, so one flag closes all four kinds
        // and each mutator asks it where the call's own name is still in hand.
        //
        // **A weak map is on the list although nothing about it is copyable**, and leaving it off
        // was the tempting mistake: `isFrozen` would then have to answer `true` for it -- the answer
        // a value with nothing to write through gets -- about a value `set` writes into happily.
        SetVal(o) -> o.frozen = true
        MapVal(o) -> o.frozen = true
        WeakMapVal(o) -> o.frozen = true
        _ -> ()

    Ok(v)
end do_freeze

// `isFrozen(v)` -- whether writing through this value is refused.
//
// **A value with nothing to write through answers TRUE**, which is `Object.isFrozen`'s answer for a
// primitive and is the honest one: the question is whether the value can change, and a number
// cannot. So `isFrozen(freeze(x))` is true for every `x`, which is the property a program reading
// this would want.
//
// **A DATA VALUE IS FROZEN AND ALWAYS WAS**, so `isFrozen(Circle(2))` is true without anybody having
// called `freeze` -- the flag is one answer whatever set it.
private do_is_frozen(args: Args, at: Span) -> Step
    if args.len() != 1 then return fault(at, s"`isFrozen` takes one value, and was given ${counted(args.len(), "argument")}")

    Ok(Bool(args.at(0) match
        Object(o) -> o.frozen
        Array(a) -> a.frozen
        Bytes(b) -> b.frozen
        SetVal(o) -> o.frozen
        MapVal(o) -> o.frozen
        WeakMapVal(o) -> o.frozen

        // A closure, a promise, a generator, a socket: each holds state a program cannot write a
        // field of, so none of them is writable and none is refused.
        _ -> true))
end do_is_frozen


// The sentence a program gets for reaching the JavaScript host through an external where there is
// none.
//
// **It says which host it is running in, which is `no_document`'s rule** -- the program is correct
// and the command is the mistake, the same source being right when it is compiled with `slate js`.
// **The declaration itself never reaches this**, so a module that binds a host value can be imported
// by a program that never asks one anything: what faults is the operation that would have to ask.
js_host_only(what: string) -> string =
    s"${what} reaches the JavaScript host, and the interpreter has no JavaScript host -- an external names a value of the host that a program compiled with `slate js` runs in"

